        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The overall goal of this project was to improve NASA risk estimates for space radiation-induced intestinal carcinogenesis. Inflammation and oxidative stress is intimately linked and both are strongly implicated in gastrointestinal (GI) carcinogenesis, and exposure to radiation results in production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) leading to oxidative stress. High levels of ROS are known to induce an apoptotic response but sustained sub-lethal oxidative stress has the potential to produce persistent modification of biomolecules including proteins, DNA, and lipids leading to alterations in their functions with adverse cellular consequences. Exposure of cells to stress is known to activate mitogenic signaling pathways such as EGFR/RAS/PI3KMAPK-related, which in turn stimulates proliferation and progression into mitosis. Persistent proliferative signals in the presence of DNA damage have been projected to propagate mutation in the progeny with survival advantage and carcinogenesis. Chronic sub-lethal oxidative stress could activate stress-sensitive kinases and transcription factors such as NFkB and AP1, and cause increased production of inflammatory cytokines. 
<p>
Although long-term effects of low linear energy transfer (low-LET) radiation such as gamma radiation on persistent oxidative stress and inflammation and how they relate to GI carcinogenesis have been investigated, information about the influence of high-LET cosmic radiation on GI cancer is scarce. Therefore, the overall goal of this proposal was to understand the effects of cosmic radiation on persistent oxidative stress and inflammation in the GI tract and how these alterations relate to GI carcinogenesis. <p>
In conjunction with the GI NASA Specialized Center of Research (NSCOR) program, the study aimed to compare the results of highly energetic charged (HZE) particle radiation to gamma radiation in mice to improve NASA’s carcinogenesis risk estimates for GI cancer in male and female astronauts through a mechanistic understanding of the underlying biological processes. The specific aim for the proposal was therefore to characterize the roles of HZE radiation-induced persistent oxidative stress and inflammatory responses in gastrointestinal carcinogenesis.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Investigators studied molecular mechanisms that could contribute to carcinogenic risk to the human GI tract. The overall aim of this project was to characterize the effects of heavy ion space radiation in triggering persistent stress and inflammatory responses with implications for GI tumorigenesis. They focused on quantitative and qualitative measurements of long-term cellular stress responses, especially oxidative, metabolic, inflammatory, and oncogenic stress responses in serum and in GI tissues after 56-Fe- and gamma-radiation. They also worked on effects of HZE radiation exposure on intestinal stem cell in relation to cellular stress. Tasks completed consist of 1) Persistent oxidative stress in intestinal epithelial cells (IEC), 2) Downregulation of autophagy, 3) Upregulation of leptin/IGF1 signaling axis, 4) Standardized intestinal stem cell (ISC) isolation protocol, 5) Measure oxidative stress in ISC, 6) Assess proliferative signal in ISC, and 7) Investigate effects of heavy ion radiation on intestinal epithelial cell (IEC) migration.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Investigators studied oxidative stress and related inflammatory responses in serum and in intestinal tissues. They also focused their attention on intestinal stem cells. They carried out three beam runs in the NASA Space Radiation Laboratory (NSRL) at Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL). They standardized intestinal stem cell isolation protocol from GFP expressing Lgr5+ (Lgr5-EGFP-IRES-creERT2) mice exposed to sham and different types of radiation using FACS-based cell sorting. Sorted stem cells were used to measure reactive oxygen species and assess mitochondrial status two months after exposure. Additionally, intestinal sections were stained for gamma H2AX, which showed persistent DNA damage even two months after 56-Fe radiation. They exposed wild type C57BL/6J mice to different types of radiation to define how heavy ion radiation exposure affects the epithelial cell migration, which is important for maintenance of intestinal homeostasis and is perturbed in intestinal pathologies including cancer. Exposed mice were intraperitoneally injected with BrdU seven days after radiation and mice were sacrificed 4, 24, and 48 hours after injection. Intestinal sections were stained for anti-BrdU and analysis showed altered cell migration after irradiation. 
<p>
No datasets exist for this study. A final report was archived.
<p>
